{"author":{"id":"337a442880d7106d509ff84f2f7fef51acce8bce88b081d4f4c3f0298b7e7941"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791580042,"nonce":"bmVw3BQ01CvAGhcYS66ji/GjPew=","message":"Decision: verify probes through an enabled-policy copy of each row (FetchContext refuses blocked policy), so robots.txt and any robots-allowed entry are still read for blocked/manual rows; the entry is never fetched when robots disallows it. Reason: JobLink bundle drift detection needs the policy-blocked joblink rows. Decision: prior robots allowance is inferred from registry policy (enabled means search and detail were allowed) because only the robots hash is stored; hash change alone is a note, newly disallowed probe paths on an enabled row is blocked. Reason: no old robots text is kept. Decision: drift writes status suspect, broken and blocked write as such, never replacing a stronger status, policy-owned statuses (blocked, manual, disabled) get only status_note, and verify clears its own flag (note prefix verify:) when the source is clean again. Reason: runner owns status. Decision: tier api rows are not probed, and a robots-closed entry on a non-enabled or disallow_all row is ok not blocked. Reason: avoids weekly false alarms for known-closed sources. Decision: robots.txt is fetched via FetchContext._request(check_robots=False) so verify can hash the body, costing one extra conditional robots request per source.","files":null}]}